"Remarks on pseudo-vertex-transitive graphs with small diameter - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012365X22001960)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; We discuss the thinness of Γ and show that the isomorphism class of the standard module of the Terwilliger algebra of Γ is determined by its intersection numbers; thus, it is pseudo-vertex-transitive; cf. ... We discuss the thinness of Γ and show that Γ is pseudo-vertex-transitive, provided that Γ is tight; cf.\nIn other words, when Γ is pseudo-vertex-transitive, it means that the isomorphism class of the standard $T(x)$-module of Γ does not depend on the base vertex x; consequently, the multiplicities of irreducible $T(x)$-modules do not depend on the base vertex x.\n\nWe recall the notion of the thinness of Γ. Let W denote an irreducible $T(x)$-module. Then W is a direct sum of nonzero spaces among ${E}_{0}^{⁎}W,{E}_{1}^{⁎}W,\\dots ,{E}_{D}^{⁎}W$, and also a direct sum of nonzero spaces among ${E}_{0}W,{E}_{1}W,\\dots ,{E}_{D}W$, where {E}_{i}${E}_{i}$ is the ith primitive idempotent of Γ. We note that the dimension of ${E}_{i}^{⁎}W$ is at most 1 for $0\\le i\\le D$ if and only if the dimension of ${E}_{i}W$ is at most 1 for $0\\le i\\le D$ [25, Lemma 3.9]; in this case, W is called thin. The graph Γ is called thin whenever every irreducible $T(x)$-module is thin for every vertex $x\\in X$.\n\nIn the present paper, we discuss the thinness and pseudo-vertex transitivity of Q-polynomial distance-regular graphs with small diameter $D\\in \\{2,3,4\\}$. For $D=2$, a distance-regular graph is strongly regular. We show that pseudo-vertex transitivity of a strongly regular graph is determined by the spectrum of its local graph; cf. Theorem 5.11. For $D\\in \\{3,4\\}$, we discuss only the distance-regular antipodal double covers. Let Γ denote a distance-regular antipodal double cover with diameter D. If $D=3$, then Γ is a Taylor graph. We discuss the thinness of Γ and show that the isomorphism class of the standard module of the Terwilliger algebra of Γ is determined by its intersection numbers; thus, it is pseudo-vertex-transitive; cf. Theorem 7.6. If $D=4$, Γ is Q-polynomial if and only if Γ is tight [16]. We discuss the thinness of Γ and show that Γ is pseudo-vertex-transitive, provided that Γ is tight; cf. Theorem 8.7. We note that bipartite and/or antipodal Q-polynomial distance-regular graphs are pseudo-vertex-transitive; cf. Proposition 6.3. In a future paper, we will discuss pseudo-vertex transitivity of general Q-polynomial distance-regular graphs with diameter $D\\ge 3$.\n\nWe remark that the thin property of an antipodal Q-polynomial double cover with $D\\in \\{3,4\\}$ plays an important role in determining pseudo-vertex transitivity. From this point of view, we could ask the following question: for $D\\ge 3$ does the thinness of a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph guarantees its pseudo-vertex transitivity? Recently, Ito and Koolen suggested the following problem:\n\nProblem 1.1 The Ito-Koolen Problem [22]\n\nClassify all thin pseudo-vertex-transitive Q-polynomial distance-regular graphs with large enough diameter.\n\nThe ultimate goal of our study is to classify thin pseudo-vertex-transitive Q-polynomial distance-regular graphs. The present paper shows that such graphs with small diameter are difficult to classify.\n\nThis paper is organized as follows. Sections 2 and 3 review some preliminaries concerning distance-regular graphs and the Terwilliger algebra. Section 4 introduces the notion of a pseudo-vertex-transitive distance-regular graph. It also contains what it means for two Q-polynomial distance-regular graphs to be T-isomorphic. Section 5 deals with the thinness of strongly regular graphs and contains a characterization of their pseudo-vertex transitivity. It also contains some examples of cospectral strong regular graphs and discusses their pseudo-vertex transitivity. Section 6 reviews some preliminaries concerning the antipodal distance-regular graphs. Section 7 deals with Taylor graphs and their thinness and pseudo-vertex transitivity. Section 8 discusses the antipodal tight graphs with $D=4$. The paper ends with a brief summary and directions for future work in Section 9.\n\nThroughout the paper, we use the following notation. Let X denote a nonempty finite set. Let ${\\mathrm{Mat}}_{X}(\\mathbb{C})$ denote the \\mathbb{C}$\\mathbb{C}$-algebra consisting of all complex matrices whose rows and columns are indexed by X. Let $V={\\mathbb{C}}^{X}$ denote the \\mathbb{C}$\\mathbb{C}$-vector space consisting of all complex column vectors with rows indexed by X. We endow V with the standard Hermitian inner product $〈u,v〉={u}^{t}\\stackrel{‾}{v}$ for $u,v\\in V$. We view V as a left module for ${\\mathrm{Mat}}_{X}(\\mathbb{C})$, called the standard module.\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCellular subalgebras of the partition algebra | CiNii Research (https://cir.nii.ac.jp/crid/1360584341840878336)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.9 years ago; Crawled: 1.2 years ago; <jats:p>We describe various diagram algebras and their representation theory using cellular algebras of Graham and Lehrer and the decomposition into half diagrams. ...     1360584341840878336 ...     10.48550/arxiv.2211.08746\n\n# Cellular subalgebras of the partition algebra\n\nDOI DOI Open Access\n\n  * Travis Scrimshaw\n    \n    Hokkaido University, Sapporo, Japan\n\n## Search this article\n\n  * CiNii Books\n\n## Description\n\n<jats:p>We describe various diagram algebras and their representation theory using cellular algebras of Graham and Lehrer and the decomposition into half diagrams. In particular, we show the diagram algebras surveyed here are all cellular algebras and parameterize their cell modules. We give a new construction to build new cellular algebras from a general cellular algebra and subalgebras of the rook Brauer a